# The doctor enforcement cohort

`DOCTOR-PWA-MULTI-DOCTOR-PILOT-1`. Read this with rule 150, which it extends
rather than replaces — everything Phase 4A says about non-destructive pilots,
preparation-is-not-activation and unreachable fleet-wide denial still holds.

## What changed

Until this sprint the pilot was one `?int` compared with `===`. "A pilot" and
"one doctor" were the same statement, and a second doctor could only be added
by going fleet-wide, which is Phase 5 and is refused. The scope is now an
explicit LIST — `AndroidDoctorEnforcementScope::pilotDoctorUserIds()` — and the
list is the only expansion shape that exists.

## The rules

**MD-R1 — Covered means named.** The cohort is an explicit allowlist of
`users.id`. No wildcard, no role name, no branch, no "all doctors", no implicit
expansion of any kind. An id is covered because somebody wrote that id down.

**MD-R2 — A pilot is not a rollout.** Expanding the cohort is never global
enforcement. `GLOBAL_ENFORCEMENT_ACTIVE` stays false, and Phase 5 remains a
separate programme with its own GO.

**MD-R3 — The ceiling lives in source control.**
`android_release.enforcement.scope.pilot_cohort_maximum` bounds how many ids
the word "pilot" may name. It is NOT in the runtime file, for the same reason
`global_permitted` is not: a bound an operator can raise on a host is not a
bound. Without it, an explicit list written out until it names every doctor is
fleet-wide denial that passes every guard watching for fleet-wide denial. A
missing or unreadable ceiling resolves to 1, never to unlimited.

**MD-R4 — Readiness before enforcement.** A doctor is provisioned before they
are covered: active account, active device, active doctor-device authorization,
and a usable credential with `user_verified=true`, `backup_eligible=false` and
`device_bound`. Adding an id to the cohort before that path exists locks a
doctor out of their own patients.

**MD-R5 — Unreadable covers nobody.** One entry that cannot be parsed voids the
ENTIRE cohort. Dropping the bad entry instead would leave a doctor silently
unenforced behind a list that still reads correctly; voiding covers nobody and
trips `armed_but_covers_nobody`, which is loud. Loud beats quiet — this class
has always accepted a loud failure as the price of never over-covering.

**MD-R6 — Scope is configuration, not provisioning.** Expanding the cohort
creates no device, authorization or credential. Contracting it revokes none.
Those are three separately revocable records and they are changed only through
their own audited product paths.

**MD-R7 — Membership is not admission.** The cohort decides that enforcement
APPLIES to a doctor. Whether they get in still needs a device-bound session,
re-asserted on every protected request. Adding ids is a way to shut a browser,
never a way to let anybody in.

**MD-R8 — The role predicate still holds.** Naming a non-doctor account in the
cohort covers nobody. A cohort is a narrowing, never a grant.

**MD-R9 — GO means the covered set equals the declared set.** Not "exactly one
doctor", and not merely the right count. A cohort of the right SIZE built from
the wrong IDS is the `users.id` / `mst_doctors.id` mix-up this report exists to
catch.

**MD-R10 — Device revoke stays terminal.** It is not a rollback mechanism and
not a routine test. Narrowing the cohort is how a doctor leaves the pilot.

**MD-R11 — Every physical ceremony is watermarked.** Take the maximum
`sys_audit_logs.id` immediately BEFORE the ceremony and count only events strictly
newer than it. Without a watermark an old login is indistinguishable from a new
one, and a ceremony can be "proven" by evidence that predates it.

**MD-R12 — Server-verified and operator-attested are different claims.** An
installed Android PWA in standalone mode sends a user agent byte-identical to
Chrome's. The server therefore CANNOT distinguish the two clients. What it can
prove is that two independent assertions occurred: separate challenge, separate
session invalidation, separate signature-counter increment. Which client produced
each is operator testimony. Record the two separately and never promote testimony
into a server fact. The server CAN distinguish two different tablets, because
their user agents differ.

**MD-R13 — A credential whose physical origin is uncertain is revoked.** The
registration URL carries the device id, so registering from the wrong tablet, or
against the wrong device id, produces a credential whose row and hardware may
disagree. Android platform authenticators report AAGUID all-zeros with attestation
`none` and a fresh counter of 0, so the database cannot tell them apart after the
fact. Ask the operator which device they were holding; if the answer is anything
other than certain, revoke. `device_bound` is worthless if the binding might name
the wrong hardware. Before registering, confirm the page names the intended device.

**MD-R14 — Cohort changes are host configuration, not a release.** Change only the
cohort key, then rebuild as the application user:
`sudo -u daengtisiams php artisan config:clear && config:cache`. Never edit the
database, never add a second override, never touch `global_permitted`. A cohort
change needs no deploy; a code change does, and that goes through
`scripts/deploy-vps-runner.sh` executed ON the VPS.

## Two mutants that are equivalent, and stay

Recorded so no future campaign re-derives them:

- Strict membership in `coversUser()`. Dropping the `true` argument changes no
  test, because `$userId` is a typed int and the cohort holds only ints. It
  stays because the equivalence is a property of the LIST, and that property is
  pinned by `MultiDoctorPilotCohortTest`.
- The set comparison in the report's verdict. Forcing it to `true` changes no
  test, because both directions are already findings and a non-empty findings
  list returns FAIL first. It stays so a suppressed or renamed finding cannot
  turn a scope covering the wrong doctors into a GO.

## Status

**The pilot is LIVE.** Cohort `[9, 15, 18]`: drg Karmila (device 3, SPN4), drg Nisa
(device 5, LDK2), drg Fiitri (device 6, ATG3). Three doctors, three branches, three
active devices, each with exactly one usable device-bound credential, each proven
by physical Chrome and installed-PWA ceremonies with live signature-counter
movement.

`GLOBAL_ENFORCEMENT_ACTIVE=false`. The ceiling is 5 and raising it is a reviewed
source change. This GO authorises these three doctors and nothing else — not a
fourth doctor, not a new device, not fleet-wide enforcement.

Note for anyone running the preparation audit: `android:phase4a-pilot-readiness`
reports FAIL on `enforcement_inactive` and will keep doing so for as long as any
pilot is live. That command judges the PREPARATION sprint, which was required to
ship enforcement off. The gate that judges a running pilot is
`android:phase4a-pilot-scope`.
